    # Body First-Level Heading

    ## Body Second-Level Heading

    ### Body Third-Level Heading

    #### Body Fourth-Level Heading

    ##### Body Fifth-Level Heading

    This paragraph verifies that
    headings in the slide body use
    the body-heading scale instead
    of the promoted slide-header
    scale. The smallest body
    heading on this slide should
    still read as a heading and
    must not become smaller than
    this paragraph text.

    # Sparse Body Autofit

    One short paragraph should
    become comfortably large
    without looking like a
    marketing splash page.

    # Dense Body Autofit

    This slide intentionally
    contains enough body content
    to force a smaller body font.
    The renderer should preserve
    readable spacing, keep
    headings predictable, and
    avoid clipping content in a
    typical fullscreen browser
    viewport.

    Autofit should treat this as
    normal slide content rather
    than a failure mode. If a
    slide is too dense even at the
    minimum font size, the
    fallback should be visually
    obvious and still allow the
    presenter to reach all
    content.

    The goal is to test the
    everyday case where a
    technical presenter adds a
    little too much detail but
    still expects the deck to
    adapt gracefully on desktop
    and laptop screens.

    # Inline Markdown

    Plain text can include
    emphasis, strong text,
    combined emphasis, inline code
    , links
    (https://example.com/deck),
    and autolinks like https:
    //example.com/autolink.

    # Lists

    - Unordered item with a short
      label.
    - Unordered item with inline
      emphasis, strong text, and
      inline code.
    - Unordered item with nested
      structure:
      - Nested unordered item.
      - Nested item with a link
        
        (https://example.com/nested)
        .

    1. Ordered item one.
    2. Ordered item two with a
       longer sentence that should
       wrap cleanly.
    3. Ordered item three.

    # Task Lists

    - [ ] Unchecked task item.
    - [x] Checked task item.
    - [ ] Task item with enough
          text to wrap across more
          than one visual line
          when the browser
          viewport is narrow.

    Task list rendering is a
    future Markdown extension in
    some contexts, so this slide
    also documents the desired
    visual target if task markers
    are supported.

    # Block Quotes

    > A short quote should read as
    > a distinct block.

    > A longer quote should wrap
    > cleanly and preserve the
    > quote treatment across
    > lines. It should not collide
    > with the slide number
    > indicator.
    >
    > - Quoted list item.
    > - Another quoted list item
    >   with strong text.

    # Code

    int main(void)
    {
        const char *boundary = "-…
        return boundary[0] ==…
    ' ? 0 : 1;
    }

    The fenced --- string above
    must not split the deck.

    # Preformatted Text

    make test
    cmdf --deck --slide-numbe…
    x fade -o deck.html testd…
    deck-corpus/comprehensive.md
    open deck.html

    Indented code should remain
    readable and should not
    participate in Markdown slide
    boundary parsing.

